<PageLayout title="About Bruce's Breads" subtitle="What this site is, and why it's built this way.">

  <p>Bruce's Breads is a sourdough baking site built by one guy — Bruce — who got a little too into artisan bread and decided the internet needed to know about it. What you'll find here is a working baker's notebook that got ambitious: real, tested sourdough recipes, the science and technique behind them written out properly, and a fair amount of personality mixed in, because a loaf of bread has never once stopped me from also wanting to tell you about the music I baked it to.</p>

  <h2>How the site is organized</h2>

  <p><strong>Recipes come first, always.</strong> Open any recipe page and the recipe card and step-by-step instructions are right at the top — ingredients, weights, baker's percentages, timing, the works. If all you want is to bake the bread, you never have to scroll past that. Everything else on the page — the ingredient history, the baking playlist tuned to each phase of the bake, the books and movies that pair with it — sits below, for whenever you're curious enough to stick around.</p>

  <p><strong>Guides go deep.</strong> Alongside the recipes is a growing library of <a href="/guides/">baking reference material</a> — starter creation and maintenance, how pH and flour and hydration actually interact, proofing and shaping, braiding, flour blending, salt timing. These aren't quick tip lists; they're written to actually teach the mechanics, the way I wished someone had explained them to me.</p>

  <p><strong>Sami is his own thing.</strong> Sami the Sourdough Sandwich is an anthropomorphic loaf who gets into adventures — Driftwood Beach, Tallulah Gorge, the Saturday farmers market, and more on the way as he travels farther from home. It's a side project for the kid in everyone, illustrated and a little ridiculous, with a real map tracking everywhere he's been.</p>

  <p><strong>Resources are the practical stuff.</strong> Printable templates for tracking your own starter and your own bakes, and a running playlist of everything that's ever soundtracked a loaf here, all in one place.</p>

  <h2>Why it's built this way</h2>

  <p>Here's the honest version: this site was built to suit <em>me</em> first. The history sections, the music, the movies and books, Sami — none of that is standard recipe-blog fare, and it's here because it's what keeps me engaged enough to keep doing this well past the point where a simple ingredient list would do. The recipe-forward layout is the compromise that makes that work for everyone else too: if you just want to bake, the extras never get in your way. If you want to fall down the rabbit hole with me, it's right there waiting.</p>

  <h2>A site that's still rising</h2>

  <p>Bruce's Breads is not a finished thing, and it's not trying to be. New recipes, new guides, new Sami stories, and the occasional structural change all show up here on no particular schedule — whenever a bake, a bit of research, or an idea is actually ready, not on a content calendar. If you check back and something looks different, that's not a bug, that's the point.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>A few things in the works</strong>, loosely, without promises on timing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Enriched doughs and laminated pastries</strong> — brioche, croissants, and their relatives, currently in active testing.</li>
      <li><strong>A deeper Guides library</strong> — flour science, flavor development, and a troubleshooting/FAQ guide pulling together the common fixes scattered across the existing guides.</li>
      <li><strong>More of Sami's travels</strong> — he's leaving Georgia for the first time soon.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

